College Event Kit Relay — Complete LLD Project Blueprint
This is the full project specification you can use as your reference before building the application in Antigravity and pushing it to GitHub.
The goal is to build a working project, not just diagrams. This specification covers the project structure, user and admin actions, booking rules, authentication, models, services, database, API, UI, error handling, testing, and deployment expectations.
1. Project overview
Project name: College Event Kit Relay
Problem statement: A college owns one event equipment kit shared by multiple clubs. Clubs need to reserve the kit for different time slots. The system must prevent conflicting reservations, enforce a 15-minute inspection gap, track the condition of the equipment, and handle missing or damaged items without silently disrupting future bookings.




One physical kit — KIT-001
- ITEM-CAM-01 — Camera
- ITEM-TRI-01 — Tripod
- ITEM-MIC-01 — Microphone



Core rules
- One kit can serve only one club at a time.
- A 15-minute inspection gap is required after a booking.
- Every required item must be inspected at checkout and return.
- Missing or damaged equipment must be tracked as an incident.
- A confirmed booking can become at risk without being automatically cancelled.
- Only authorized users can perform protected actions.

2. Project objectives
The application must:
1. Allow club representatives to register, log in, and manage their own bookings.
2. Display available time slots for the physical kit.
3. Validate booking requests and prevent time conflicts.
4. Enforce the 15-minute gap between reservations.
5. Allow authorized organizers to check out and receive returned equipment.
6. Record the condition of the camera, tripod, and microphone individually.
7. Report and resolve equipment incidents.
8. Identify future bookings affected by equipment problems.
9. Notify affected clubs and administrators.
10. Maintain an audit trail of important actions.
11. Protect user information and enforce role-based access.
12. Provide automated tests for normal and failure scenarios.
3. Actors and permissions
Club representative
A club representative can:
- Register and log in.
- View kit details and availability.
- Create a booking for their own club.
- View their club's bookings and booking details.
- Cancel an eligible booking according to the cancellation policy.
- View booking status and any disruption warnings.
- View their notifications.
- View their club's booking history.
They cannot manage equipment inventory, check out the kit, resolve incidents, manage users, or view other clubs' private bookings.
Equipment organizer
An equipment organizer can:
- Log in to the organizer dashboard.
- View all bookings and the daily schedule.
- View the kit's readiness and individual equipment status.
- Perform checkout and return inspections.
- Record item conditions, missing items, and damage.
- Create and update incidents.
- Record recovered or replacement equipment.
- Resolve incidents after verifying the outcome.
- Identify affected bookings and send warnings.
- Cancel or reschedule bookings when authorized, with a recorded reason.
- View relevant audit and notification history.
Administrator
The administrator has organizer permissions plus administrative permissions, such as:
- Create, deactivate, or reactivate clubs.
- Create or deactivate user accounts.
- Assign roles and link representatives to clubs.
- Configure permitted booking durations and cancellation policies.
- View system-wide audit records.
- Manage application-level settings.
Do not allow a user to grant themselves administrator privileges.
System
The application automatically:
- Validates booking dates and times.
- Checks the inspection gap.
- Prevents simultaneous conflicting bookings.
- Updates booking and equipment states.
- Identifies potentially affected future bookings.
- Creates notification and audit records.
- Retries failed notification delivery where appropriate.
The system is the software itself, not a separate human account.
