5. Complete booking rules
These are business rules. They must be enforced by backend services and the database where possible, not only by frontend validation.
5.1 Time validation
- Start time must be earlier than end time.
- Reject bookings in the past.
- Define the allowed booking duration and opening hours as configurable policies if the college has restrictions.
- Use consistent timestamp storage, preferably UTC, and display times in the college's local timezone.
- Reject invalid or ambiguous date/time inputs.
5.2 Booking conflicts
A booking conflicts if it overlaps an existing active reservation or violates the required gap after the previous booking.
Example:
- Club A books 2:00–3:00 PM.
- Club B requests 3:10 PM: reject.
- Club B requests 3:14 PM: reject.
- Club B requests 3:15 PM: passes the gap rule.
- Club B requests 3:30 PM: passes the gap rule.
The final two requests can still be rejected if another booking, operational restriction, or validation rule prevents them.
Use the same availability logic for every booking request, including requests made by administrators. If an administrator needs to override a rule, define an explicit, permission-controlled override policy and record it in the audit log.
5.3 Concurrency
Two users may request the same time slot at nearly the same moment.
The backend must perform conflict validation and reservation creation as one atomic operation, using a transaction and suitable database locking or concurrency control. A simple “check first, save later” implementation is not enough.
5.4 Booking lifecycle
Keep the booking lifecycle explicit:
- CONFIRMED — reservation exists.
- CHECKED_OUT — kit has been handed over.
- COMPLETED — return process is complete.
- CANCELLED — reservation has been cancelled.
If the college later requires approval, add a separate PENDING_APPROVAL state. Do not add it unless approval is actually required.
Track disruption risk separately, such as with risk_reason or an explicit risk field. A confirmed booking may be at risk if equipment becomes unavailable.
5.5 Checkout and return
- Only an authorized organizer can perform checkout and return.
- Checkout must refer to an eligible booking.
- All required equipment must be inspected.
- A missing or unusable item blocks handover.
- Return inspection must record the condition of every item.
- An incident must be created when equipment is missing or damaged.
- The kit must not become ready solely because 15 minutes have elapsed.
- Readiness requires the required inspection and all equipment to be usable.
5.6 Cancellation and disruptions
- Club representatives may cancel only eligible bookings belonging to their club.
- Administrators may cancel bookings only according to their permissions and policy.
- Record the cancellation reason and actor.
- Do not automatically cancel all future bookings when an incident occurs.
- Flag potentially affected bookings and notify the relevant people.
- After recovery or replacement, verify kit readiness before clearing the disruption.
6. Authentication and security design
Use role-based access control (RBAC).
Authentication: Who is the user?
Authorization: What is this user allowed to do?
Recommended security requirements:
- Store passwords using a modern password-hashing algorithm.
- Never store passwords in plain text.
- Use secure sessions or signed, short-lived access tokens.
- Use HTTPS in deployment.
- Validate authorization on every protected API request.
- Check booking ownership using the authenticated user's identity.
- Do not trust a role, user ID, or club ID supplied by the browser without verification.
- Restrict user and role management to administrators.
- Rate-limit login attempts.
- Keep secrets in environment variables rather than GitHub.
- Use appropriate cookie security settings if cookie-based authentication is selected.
- Configure CORS narrowly for the frontend's actual origin.
- Avoid returning password hashes or private account fields in API responses.
For the first implementation, choose one authentication approach and implement it consistently. Do not mix session authentication and JWT authentication without a clear reason.
7. Domain models and class responsibilities
Models represent the things in the system. Each model should contain the data it owns and, where appropriate, small business methods.
User
Represents a person who can sign in.
- user_id
- name
- email
- password_hash
- role
- club_id (optional for staff)
- is_active
- created_at
Responsibilities: represent the account, its assigned role, and its relationship with a club.
Club
Represents a college club.
- club_id
- name
- contact_email
- is_active
- created_at
Responsibilities: identify the club and determine whether it can make new bookings.
Booking
Represents one reservation of the kit.
- booking_id
- club_id
- kit_id
- start_time
- end_time
- status
- risk_reason (optional)
- created_at
- cancelled_at (optional)
- cancellation_reason (optional)
Responsibilities: store reservation details, support permitted lifecycle transitions, and preserve cancellation information.
EventKit
Represents the physical equipment kit.
- kit_id
- name
- description
Responsibilities: identify the kit and provide access to its equipment. Its readiness should be calculated from item conditions, reservations, inspections, and operational restrictions rather than relying on a manually maintained flag alone.
EquipmentItem
Represents one item in the kit.
- item_id
- kit_id
- name
- status
- condition_notes
- last_inspected_at
Responsibilities: track the identity, condition, and operational status of an item.
The three initial items are the camera, tripod, and microphone. Avoid hard-coding their names into the business logic so additional equipment can be supported later.
InspectionRecord
Represents one checkout or return inspection.
- inspection_id
- booking_id
- inspector_id
- inspection_type
- inspected_at
- notes
Responsibilities: record who performed the inspection, when it happened, and whether it was a checkout or return inspection.
InspectionItemResult
Represents the result for one item during an inspection.
- result_id
- inspection_id
- item_id
- condition
- notes
Responsibilities: store the condition of each inspected item separately. An inspection must not be marked complete if required items have not been checked.
Incident
Represents an equipment problem or disruption.
- incident_id
- item_id
- related_booking_id (optional)
- reported_by
- description
- status
- created_at
- resolution_notes
- resolved_at (optional)
Responsibilities: track the problem, recovery or replacement, and verified resolution.
Notification
Represents a message intended for a user.
- notification_id
- user_id
- message
- type
- delivery_status
- created_at
- sent_at (optional)
- retry_count
Responsibilities: retain notification history and track delivery attempts.
AuditLog
Represents a record of an important action.
- audit_id
- actor_id
- action
- entity_type
- entity_id
- timestamp
- details
Responsibilities: provide traceability for important user and admin operations.
Audit records should not be silently changed or deleted through ordinary application features.
Enums
Use explicit enums for roles, booking status, equipment status, inspection type, item condition, incident status, and notification status.
Examples of equipment status:
- AVAILABLE
- MISSING
- DAMAGED
- UNDER_REPAIR
Examples of inspection type:
- CHECKOUT
- RETURN
Keep item condition, item operational status, booking status, and booking risk distinct. They represent different concepts.
